1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,910
A model's near-perfect

2
00:00:01,910 --> 00:00:03,518
on the data it learned from

3
00:00:03,518 --> 00:00:04,887
then fails on new data.

4
00:00:05,237 --> 00:00:07,373
Usually that's not a bug in the code.

5
00:00:07,373 --> 00:00:09,335
It's a problem called overfitting.

6
00:00:17,667 --> 00:00:19,455
Overfitting is when a model learns

7
00:00:19,455 --> 00:00:21,084
its practice data too literally

8
00:00:21,084 --> 00:00:21,820
flukes and all

9
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:23,345
so it does worse on new data.

10
00:00:23,695 --> 00:00:25,632
Picture a student who memorizes

11
00:00:25,632 --> 00:00:28,631
every answer in the workbook, misprints included

12
00:00:28,631 --> 00:00:30,256
and scores 100 every time.

13
00:00:30,606 --> 00:00:31,581
But on the exam

14
00:00:31,581 --> 00:00:33,791
the questions change just a little

15
00:00:33,791 --> 00:00:34,701
and he's lost.

16
00:00:35,051 --> 00:00:37,014
The workbook is the model's data

17
00:00:37,014 --> 00:00:39,530
and the misprints are what we call noise.

18
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:41,790
When a model memorizes the noise

19
00:00:41,790 --> 00:00:44,714
as if it were a real pattern, that's overfitting.

20
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:50,030
Let's see it happen.

21
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:52,244
These 10 points are our training data

22
00:00:52,244 --> 00:00:53,621
our practice questions.

23
00:00:53,971 --> 00:00:55,732
They roughly follow a pattern

24
00:00:55,732 --> 00:00:57,310
but each one is nudged off

25
00:00:57,310 --> 00:00:58,889
by a bit of random wobble.

26
00:00:58,889 --> 00:01:00,407
That wobble is the noise.

27
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:02,368
The model's job is to find

28
00:01:02,368 --> 00:01:04,598
that pattern and predict new points.

29
00:01:04,948 --> 00:01:06,918
The first model is too simple.

30
00:01:06,918 --> 00:01:09,085
It can only draw a straight line.

31
00:01:09,435 --> 00:01:11,874
The farther a point is from the line

32
00:01:11,874 --> 00:01:13,432
the bigger the mistake.

33
00:01:13,432 --> 00:01:15,668
The average mistake is the error.

34
00:01:16,018 --> 00:01:17,379
The line can't even get

35
00:01:17,379 --> 00:01:19,095
the practice questions right.

36
00:01:19,095 --> 00:01:20,692
That's called underfitting.

37
00:01:21,042 --> 00:01:23,267
The second model is a gentle curve.

38
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:24,602
It misses some points

39
00:01:24,602 --> 00:01:26,700
but it catches the overall trend.

40
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:28,830
The third is really complicated

41
00:01:28,830 --> 00:01:29,979
twisting and turning

42
00:01:29,979 --> 00:01:31,472
through every single point

43
00:01:31,472 --> 00:01:33,195
so its practice error is zero.

44
00:01:33,545 --> 00:01:34,985
So which one do you think

45
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:36,714
will do best on new questions?

46
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:41,088
Now we test them on 6

47
00:01:41,088 --> 00:01:42,766
points they've never seen.

48
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:44,767
That's the test data, our exam.

49
00:01:45,117 --> 00:01:46,505
The second model stays

50
00:01:46,505 --> 00:01:47,767
close to all of them

51
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:48,777
so it does best.

52
00:01:49,127 --> 00:01:51,336
The third zigzags between the practice

53
00:01:51,336 --> 00:01:53,835
points and misses some new points by a lot.

54
00:01:54,185 --> 00:01:56,153
Its exam error is more than twenty

55
00:01:56,153 --> 00:01:57,600
times the second model's.

56
00:01:57,950 --> 00:02:00,155
That's because it memorized the noise

57
00:02:00,155 --> 00:02:01,287
and noise is random

58
00:02:01,287 --> 00:02:03,551
so it doesn't repeat on new questions.

59
00:02:03,551 --> 00:02:04,683
That's overfitting.

60
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:06,932
Underfitting is easy to spot:

61
00:02:06,932 --> 00:02:08,568
it does badly on practice

62
00:02:08,568 --> 00:02:09,812
and exam questions.

63
00:02:10,162 --> 00:02:11,658
Overfitting is sneakier

64
00:02:11,658 --> 00:02:12,958
because its practice

65
00:02:12,958 --> 00:02:14,454
score can look amazing.

66
00:02:14,454 --> 00:02:16,665
Here, its practice error was zero.

67
00:02:20,767 --> 00:02:22,554
Here's another way to see it:

68
00:02:22,554 --> 00:02:24,587
think of learning as compression.

69
00:02:24,937 --> 00:02:27,315
To remember these 10 points exactly

70
00:02:27,315 --> 00:02:29,354
you'd need to store 10 numbers

71
00:02:29,354 --> 00:02:30,781
one height per point.

72
00:02:31,131 --> 00:02:33,320
The second curve takes just 4 numbers

73
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:35,036
yet stays close to the points

74
00:02:35,036 --> 00:02:37,817
and predicts new ones well. That's compression.

75
00:02:38,167 --> 00:02:39,665
The third curve needs 10

76
00:02:39,665 --> 00:02:41,662
numbers to go through 10 points.

77
00:02:42,012 --> 00:02:43,609
Ten numbers for ten points

78
00:02:43,609 --> 00:02:45,083
is no compression at all

79
00:02:45,083 --> 00:02:46,741
so the noise got saved too.

80
00:02:47,091 --> 00:02:48,925
Good compression keeps the pattern

81
00:02:48,925 --> 00:02:50,274
and throws out the noise.

82
00:02:50,274 --> 00:02:52,486
Squeeze too hard, like that straight line

83
00:02:52,486 --> 00:02:54,050
and you lose the pattern too.

84
00:02:58,167 --> 00:02:59,588
Take Google Flu Trends.

85
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:01,688
Google once used people's searches

86
00:03:01,688 --> 00:03:03,665
to estimate what share of doctor

87
00:03:03,665 --> 00:03:05,766
visits involved flu-like symptoms.

88
00:03:06,116 --> 00:03:08,962
At first, it sifted 50 million search terms

89
00:03:08,962 --> 00:03:11,410
to match about 1,100 flu data points.

90
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,717
With far more terms than data points

91
00:03:13,717 --> 00:03:15,239
it was bound to pick up some

92
00:03:15,239 --> 00:03:17,304
that just happened to spike in winter.

93
00:03:17,654 --> 00:03:20,213
Researchers said this was overfitting.

94
00:03:20,213 --> 00:03:21,830
It was part flu detector

95
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:23,244
part winter detector.

96
00:03:23,594 --> 00:03:25,627
Later versions missed too.

97
00:03:25,627 --> 00:03:27,426
In 100 out of 108 weeks

98
00:03:27,426 --> 00:03:29,146
they guessed too high.

99
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:35,785
How do you spot overfitting and stop it?

100
00:03:35,785 --> 00:03:37,485
Here are three common ways.

101
00:03:37,835 --> 00:03:40,942
First, set aside some data as a mock exam

102
00:03:40,942 --> 00:03:42,761
called a validation set.

103
00:03:43,111 --> 00:03:44,533
Use it to pick your model

104
00:03:44,533 --> 00:03:46,127
and catch overfitting early.

105
00:03:46,127 --> 00:03:48,175
Save the real exam for the very end.

106
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:49,325
To be honest

107
00:03:49,325 --> 00:03:51,126
in our experiment we picked

108
00:03:51,126 --> 00:03:52,993
the model by its exam score.

109
00:03:52,993 --> 00:03:55,193
On a real project, you shouldn't.

110
00:03:55,543 --> 00:03:57,352
Second, collect more data

111
00:03:57,352 --> 00:03:58,799
and more varied data

112
00:03:58,799 --> 00:04:01,258
so noise and coincidences are less

113
00:04:01,258 --> 00:04:03,284
likely to pass for patterns.

114
00:04:03,634 --> 00:04:05,554
Third, give the model a rule:

115
00:04:05,554 --> 00:04:08,136
overly complicated curves get penalized

116
00:04:08,136 --> 00:04:09,990
so it won't chase the noise.

117
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:11,910
That's called regularization.

118
00:04:13,433 --> 00:04:15,764
Overfitting is memorizing the misprints

119
00:04:15,764 --> 00:04:17,976
and mistaking them for real patterns.

120
00:04:18,326 --> 00:04:19,681
So whenever you feel like

121
00:04:19,681 --> 00:04:21,037
you totally get something

122
00:04:21,037 --> 00:04:22,826
try a question you've never seen.

123
00:04:23,176 --> 00:04:24,404
And in the next episode

124
00:04:24,404 --> 00:04:26,433
we'll get into reinforcement learning.
